arXiv:2602.22406v1 [cs.AI] 25 Feb 2026</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line ltx_leqno">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Towards Autonomous Memory Agents</h1>
<span class="ltx_pubnotes ltx_pubnotes_meta"><span class="ltx_pubnotes_content"><span class="ltx_pubnote ltx_role_doi"><span class="ltx_note_name">DOI: </span><a href="https://doi.org/XXXXXXX.XXXXXXX">XXXXXXX.XXXXXXX</a></span><span class="ltx_pubnote ltx_role_conference"><span class="ltx_note_name">Conference: </span>ACM Conference; Month 2026; Location</span><span class="ltx_pubnote ltx_role_isbn"><span class="ltx_note_name">ISBN: </span>978-1-4503-XXXX-X/2018/06</span></span></span>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Xinle Wu, Rui Zhang, Mustafa Anis Hussain, Yao Lu
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_affiliation_institution">National University of Singapore</span>, <span id="id2" class="ltx_text ltx_affiliation_country">Singapore</span>
</span></span></span></span></div>
<div class="ltx_dates">2026</div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent memory agents improve LLMs by extracting experiences and conversation history into an external storage. This enables low-overhead context assembly and online memory update without expensive LLM training. However, existing solutions remain <em id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">passive and reactive</em>; memory growth is bounded by information that happens to be available, while memory agents seldom seek external inputs in uncertainties.
We propose <em id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">autonomous</em> memory agents that actively acquire, validate, and curate knowledge at a minimum cost. U-Mem materializes this idea via (i) a cost-aware knowledge-extraction cascade that escalates from cheap self/teacher signals to tool-verified research and, only when needed, expert feedback, and (ii) semantic-aware Thompson sampling to balance exploration and exploitation over memories and mitigate cold-start bias.
On both verifiable and non-verifiable benchmarks, U-Mem consistently beats prior memory baselines and can surpass RL-based optimization, improving HotpotQA (Qwen2.5-7B) by 14.6 points and AIME25 (Gemini-2.5-flash) by 7.33 points.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Keywords: </h6>Large Language Model, Memory, Agent, Thompson Sampling
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large language models (LLMs) nowadays empower AI agents to achieve higher and broader goals <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, but deployed systems from research and industry are often ineffective in remembering user interactions, preferences, or domain knowledge beyond the given context window <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hendrycks et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. This knowledge discontinuity forces AI agents to repeatedly ask known facts, past mistakes, and recompute answers; this clearly results in increased costs and degraded user experience <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yin et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. A recent way to address this problem to improve AI agents with experiences is to update the model parameters via supervised fine-tuning (SFT) or reinforcement learning (RL) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qi et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qian et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Luo et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. However, doing so in practice is often too expensive and operationally infeasible <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024</a>)</cite>; besides, many foundation models are closed-source or difficult to update without regressions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Comanici et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>. This motivates non-parametric solutions that can improve AI agents without touching the model weights <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Agrawal et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dong et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shinn et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This has driven the recent interest in <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">memory agents</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chhikara et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cao et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2026</a>)</cite>: instead of updating model weights, the agent extracts critical experiences from the conversation history, user preferences, task outcomes, domain knowledge, and reasoning patterns, and puts them into an external memory store <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou and Han, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nan et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Patel and Patel, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Meanwhile, it retrieves and assembles relevant memories at query time to guide future decisions. This non-parametric, memory-based solution can be deployed on closed-source models with a low overhead and enables memory update when new experiences arrive <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rasmussen et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kong et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang and Chen, 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Despite this progress, existing memory agents remain largely <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">passive</em>. Their memory growth and learning are typically bounded by whatever information happens to appear from user interaction. Some recent solutions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cao et al., 2025</a>)</cite> additionally learn policies for memory operations for what to store, what to forget, and what to retrieve; this can even be done using RL-style objectives to optimize these choices <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yan et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ma et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>. Yet, the memory operations are still reactive, where the agent does not actively seek external inputs when it encounters uncertainty, detects a failure mode, or identifies an opportunity to expand its knowledge base. In practice, there is abundant external data that can be harvested with minimal user frictions, including but not limited to user follow-up questions and corrections, more powerful teacher LLMs, tool-verified data and procedures, deep-research pipelines, and structured self-reflection over successes and failures. It is notable that exploiting these signals does not require human efforts in a traditional sense; instead, it can be framed as actions by the agent’s own autonomy in actively collecting, validating and making use of the available information.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We argue that <em id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">memory agents should be autonomous</em> in how they actively acquire, validate, and curate knowledge. As shown in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Towards Autonomous Memory Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, an autonomous memory agent goes beyond passive data logging and retrieval: it decides when additional information is needed, where to obtain it (e.g., from a teacher, tools, or research), and is aware of the cost that is going to incur. This clearly distinguishes from recent art in self-evolving agents that are mostly passive. An autonomous memory agent can still evolve over time without requiring constant human annotation; this enables personalization and domain adaptation in real applications. Nevertheless, autonomy does not preclude human supervision; rather, it treats human experts as a last resort, consulted only when cheaper knowledge sources fail to provide sufficient confidence.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.22406v1/figures/intro.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/182;" width="476" height="182" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1. </span>Passive vs. autonomous memory agents.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S1.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.22406v1/figures/overview.png" id="S1.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:333/144;" width="333" height="144" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2. </span>Overview of U-Mem.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To realize this vision, we face new challenges. (1) <em id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Cost-awareness</em>. We note that, external knowledge, such as teacher LLMs, tool usage, research, is associated with different costs; the agent must decide who to query and when escalation is worth it. These issues are amplified in distribution shifts and cold-starts, where there is little feedback history to decide if new memories can be crucial for emerging needs. (2) <em id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Generalization</em>. Achieving high accuracy and generalization at the same time is non-trivial. The agent should provide a good level of accuracy across both <em id="S1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">verifiable</em> tasks, i.e., with objective checks, and <em id="S1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">non-verifiable tasks</em>, i.e., where correctness is ambiguous and feedback is model- or preference-related.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we take a preliminary step toward this direction and propose <em id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">U-Mem</em>, the first memory agent that aims for autonomous knowledge acquisition and utility-driven update under cost constraints. To address the technical challenges, U-Mem leverages a cost-aware <em id="S1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">knowledge extraction cascade</em> that progressively harvests correct knowledge by escalating supervision only when necessary; the cascade starts from inexpensive self-reflection and more powerful teacher models, moves to tool-augmented verification and deep research when needed, and consults human experts as a last resort. To address the cold-start and adaptation issue, we develop <em id="S1.p6.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">semantic-aware Thompson sampling</em> to leverage feedback from downstream queries to improve the memory retrieval and utilization over time. By combining semantic relevance with posterior sampling over memory utility, the agent can systematically exploit proven memories while still exploring new ones.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate the effectiveness of autonomous memory agents, we conduct extensive experiments on diverse benchmarks with open-weight and proprietary models, spanning verifiable reasoning (e.g., AIME) and non-verifiable generation (e.g., AdvancedIF).
Results demonstrate that <span id="S1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">U-Mem</span> consistently outperforms state-of-the-art memory baselines and, remarkably, matches or exceeds expensive Reinforcement Learning (RL) baselines.
For instance, <span id="S1.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">U-Mem</span> boosts the performance of Qwen2.5-7B on HotpotQA by over 14%, and further enhances strong proprietary models like Gemini-2.5-flash by 7.3% on AIME25.
These findings validate the efficacy of autonomous memory acquisition and establish <span id="S1.p7.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">U-Mem</span> as a robust, cost-effective method for evolving agents in dynamic deployment scenarios.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Contributions.</span> We summarize our main contributions as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:0.16in;">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Unlike memory agents that are often passive, we propose autonomous memory agents that proactively collect external knowledge and evidence to grow and refine long-term memory.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose a cost-aware knowledge extraction cascade that turns both successes and failures into durable memory, adaptively escalating supervision (teacher, tools, deep research, human) to maximize correctness. We also develop semantic-aware Thompson sampling to balance exploration and exploitation and integrate semantic relevance with learned utility.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate autonomous memory agents across both verifiable and non-verifiable tasks, demonstrating that autonomy can substantially extend the capability boundary of memory-based agent evolution under realistic budget constraints.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reinforcement learning for LLMs</span>
is a standard paradigm in model fine-tuning and post-training alignment. RL training techniques have evolved from Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schulman et al., 2017</a>)</cite> to more stable, direct techniques like DPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rafailov et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and the efficient, critic-free GRPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
Methodology-wise, the training approaches diverge by feedback types: <span id="S2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">verifiable</span> domains (e.g., math) often utilize ground-truth outcome supervision (RLVR) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, whereas <span id="S2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">non-verifiable</span> tasks often rely on preference models or AI feedback <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. While being effective, these paradigms remain computationally expensive due to the iterative parameter updates.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLMs with a memory bank</span>.
Existing research on LLM long-term memory can be categorized into two streams: dialogue memory, and reasoning trajectory memory.
The former focuses on constructing a memory bank and extracting user profiles, preferences, and factual context from multi-session dialogues to enhance personalization and consistency.
For instance, A-Mem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2025</a>)</cite> builds an agentic memory that stores long interactions as structured notes and continuously links and evolves them into an interconnected memory network for better long-term recall. Mem0 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chhikara et al., 2025</a>)</cite> proposes a scalable memory pipeline that extracts, consolidates/updates, and retrieves salient information from multi-session dialogues to support efficient long-term context recall.
While effective for open-ended chat, these methods primarily address what the user said, rather than how to solve complex problems.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Self-evolving memory agents</span>.
More relevant to our work is the second stream, which aims to distill transferable knowledge from the agent’s reasoning trajectories. ReasoningBank <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2025</a>)</cite> leverages test-time scaling to sample multiple candidate paths, synthesizing memories by contrasting self-generated successful and failed trajectories.
ReMe <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cao et al., 2025</a>)</cite> proposes a framework for agent evolution that integrates multi-faceted experience distillation, context-adaptive reuse, and utility-based refinement.
A critical limitation of these approaches is their dependency on the LLM’s intrinsic capability; they presume the model can eventually generate a correct solution via repeated sampling.
In essence, these systems remain self-contained and passive in knowledge harvesting; their knowledge growth is strictly limited to the model’s pretraining data distribution. They are not designed to autonomously seek external knowledge when intrinsic reasoning fails, thus having "hard failures" where the model consistently makes errors.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Our ideas: autonomous memory agents</span>. Unlike off-the-shelf post-training and memory solutions that are often <span id="S2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">passive</span> and <span id="S2.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">cost–agnostic</span>, <span id="S2.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">U-Mem</span> represents a shift towards <em id="S2.p4.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">autonomous</em> memory agents: (1) Our framework is <span id="S2.p4.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">active</span> to autonomously decide when to transcend internal limitations to acquire high-fidelity external knowledge from tools or even experts, and dynamically learns which memories are valuable through semantic-aware exploration. (2) On the other hand, our solution is also <span id="S2.p4.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">cost-aware</span> in both compute and data samples. Recent work such as MemRL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2026</a>)</cite> and Memento <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2025</a>)</cite> leverages query feedback to optimize the memory retrieval, but they overlook cold-start with new memories and various task difficulties. <span id="S2.p4.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">U-Mem</span> aims to address these issues.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Methods</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>Problem Formulation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We formalize the continuous improvement of an LLM agent as a streaming decision process. Consider a stream of tasks <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}=\{q_{1},q_{2},\dots,q_{T}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}=\{q_{1},q_{2},\dots,q_{T}\}</annotation></semantics></math> arriving sequentially.
The agent is parameterized by a frozen LLM backbone <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> and a dynamic memory store <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
At each time step <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, the system state is defined as <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{t}=\langle\theta,\mathcal{M}_{t}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{t}=\langle\theta,\mathcal{M}_{t}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>. Unlike traditional Reinforcement Learning (RL) which updates <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, our framework keeps <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> fixed and evolves <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> to maximize performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The interaction workflow for a query <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> proceeds as follows:</p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:0.2in;">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Retrieval:</em> The agent retrieves a subset of relevant memories <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{t}=\mathcal{R}(q_{t},\mathcal{M}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{t}=\mathcal{R}(q_{t},\mathcal{M}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> based on the current memory state.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Inference:</em> Conditioned on the retrieved context, the frozen LLM generates a reasoning trajectory <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="y_{t}\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|q_{t},C_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t}\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|q_{t},C_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Feedback:</em> The environment provides a scalar feedback signal <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{t}=\text{Eval}(y_{t},q_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Eval</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{t}=\text{Eval}(y_{t},q_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>. We consider a unified feedback formulation covering both <span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">verifiable</span> tasks (where <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is a ground-truth binary outcome) and <span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">non-verifiable</span> tasks (where <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is a preference score from an external judge).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Memory Evolution:</em> Based on the trajectory <math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and feedback <math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, the memory extraction module <math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> distills new insights to update the state: <math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{t+1}\leftarrow\text{Update}(\mathcal{M}_{t},\mathcal{E}(q_{t},y_{t},r_{t}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext>Update</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{t+1}\leftarrow\text{Update}(\mathcal{M}_{t},\mathcal{E}(q_{t},y_{t},r_{t}))</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Objective.</span>
Our goal is to design an optimal retrieval policy <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> and extraction mechanism <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> that maximize the cumulative reward <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{t=1}^{T}r_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{t=1}^{T}r_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> over the task stream.
This formulation frames memory management as a dual problem to parameter optimization: maximizing long-term utility through experience accumulation rather than weight updates.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation Protocol.</span>
To rigorously evaluate the "learning-by-using" capability, we adopt a <span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Train-Test</span> protocol.
The agent first processes a <span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Training Stream</span> where it is allowed to evolve <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> continuously.
Performance is then reported on a held-out <span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Test Set</span>, where the memory state is frozen (<math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{test}=\mathcal{M}_{final}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{test}=\mathcal{M}_{final}</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2. </span>Overview of <span id="S3.SS2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">U-Mem</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Towards Autonomous Memory Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, U-Mem enables continuous, training-free evolution via a unified <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Retrieve-Infer-Evolve</span> cycle. The workflow commences with a task router (instantiated by the frozen LLM backbone) that directs incoming queries to verifiable or non-verifiable streams. In the <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Retrieval</em> phase, we employ Semantic-Aware Thompson Sampling (SA-CTS) to select memories by balancing semantic similarity with learned utility distributions. Subsequently, the agent performs <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Inference</em> using the enhanced prompt. Finally, during <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Memory Evolution</em>, a Cost-Aware Cascade (for verifiable tasks) or pairwise comparison (for non-verifiable tasks) distills experiences into memories, while environmental feedback updates memory utility posteriors to refine future retrieval without parameter updates.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3. </span>Memory for Verifiable Tasks</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In verifiable domains (e.g., mathematical reasoning, code generation), we posit that the substantial expansion of an agent’s capability boundary stems from learning from failures rather than merely reinforcing successes. To this end, we introduce a Cost-Aware Memory Extraction Cascade to adaptively distill high-quality corrective insights from failure trajectories. For memory retrieval, we propose Semantic-Aware Thompson Sampling (SA-CTS), a utility-driven retrieval mechanism designed to filter noise and precisely surface the most helpful memories for current reasoning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Memory Extraction Cascade</span>.

Standard memory approaches often prioritize extracting insights from successful trajectories. However, we argue that the most capability-expanding signals lie in failures.
Extracting memories from failures is non-trivial. Naive self-reflection on incorrect trajectories often leads to <span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">hallucinated attribution</span>, as the agent lacks the knowledge to identify its own errors. Recent work like ReasoningBank <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2025</a>)</cite> attempts to mitigate this via parallel sampling (generating <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> trajectories). However, this fails when the model is fundamentally weak on a task, resulting in <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> incorrect trajectories and no valid reference for learning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To guarantee high-quality extraction, we introduce a <em id="S3.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Cost-Aware Cascade</em>. The core principle is that effective reflection requires a <span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">correct reference trajectory</span> (<math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{ref}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{ref}</annotation></semantics></math>) for contrastive analysis. Upon generating a failure trajectory <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{fail}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{fail}</annotation></semantics></math>, we escalate the request for <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{ref}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{ref}</annotation></semantics></math> through a hierarchy of increasing cost and reliability:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S3.I2" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:0.2in;">
<li id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Level 1: Teacher LLM.</em> We first query a stronger model. If successful, its output serves as <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{ref}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{ref}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Level 2: Tool-Augmented Teacher.</em> If the teacher model fails, we equip it with external tools (e.g., a code interpreter), which have been proven to significantly improve the correctness of LLMs.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Level 3: Human Expert (Simulated).</em> As a last resort, we request a solution from a high-authority source, ideally a human expert. While expensive, this ensures the distillation of high-value, capability-expanding knowledge. In our experimental evaluation, to ensure scalability and reproducibility, we employ <span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Gemini-3-pro-preview</span> as a proxy for human experts.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Once <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{ref}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{ref}</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained, the agent performs Contrastive Reflection by analyzing the divergence between <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{fail}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{fail}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{ref}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{ref}</annotation></semantics></math>. The distilled insights are formalized into a structured memory schema <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=\langle\textit{Title},\textit{Description},\textit{Content}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Title</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Description</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Content</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=\langle\textit{Title},\textit{Description},\textit{Content}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> to support precise retrieval . We categorize these memories into two types:</p>
<ul id="S3.I3" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:0.2in;">
<li id="S3.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Global Procedural Memory:</em> A high-level recipe summarizing the correct workflow derived from <math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{ref}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{ref}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., "For geometry proofs involving centroids, first apply the median property, then establish the segment ratios…").</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Local Corrective Memory:</em> Specific error-correction rules derived from the divergence point (e.g., "When calculating variance, do not square the sum directly; instead, calculate the mean of squares…").</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.2" class="ltx_p">If the agent succeeds initially, we bypass the cascade and directly extract Global Procedural Memory to reinforce the successful behavior.
We maintain separate stores for global and local memories, employing a flat storage structure.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Semantic-Aware Thompson Sampling for Retrieval</span>.

Existing memory frameworks typically rely on dense vector retrieval based on semantic similarity. While effective for recall, this approach often retrieves "relevant noise"—memories that are semantically related to the query but offer no substantial aid to the reasoning process. Recent works like MemRL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2026</a>)</cite> attempt to mitigate this by learning a scalar utility value for each memory based on environmental feedback (e.g., task success).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">However, we identify two critical limitations in current utility-based retrieval paradigms such as MemRL:</p>
<ul id="S3.I4" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:0.2in;">
<li id="S3.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I4.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Cold-Start Inequity (The Exploration Bias):</em> In streaming settings, newly created memories have not yet received feedback and thus lack reliable utility estimates (often undefined or conservatively initialized). Under deterministic greedy ranking, such memories are rarely retrieved, which prevents them from being evaluated and updated. This creates a self-reinforcing loop where older memories—having accumulated historical feedback—dominate retrieval, even when some new memories could be highly valuable.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I4.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Reward Bias via Task Difficulty:</em> MemRL relies on absolute rewards as feedback. This introduces significant noise: a simple task may yield a positive reward even with irrelevant memories (false positive utility), while a complex task may result in failure despite retrieving useful memories (false negative utility). This confounds intrinsic task difficulty with memory efficacy.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">To address these challenges, we propose <em id="S3.SS3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Semantic-Aware Thompson Sampling (SA-CTS)</em>. Instead of maintaining a scalar point estimate, we model the utility of each memory <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> as a Gaussian distribution <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(\mu_{i},\sigma_{i}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(\mu_{i},\sigma_{i}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, representing the estimated utility and the <span id="S3.SS3.p8.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">epistemic uncertainty</span>, respectively. This probabilistic formulation fundamentally resolves the exploration dilemma: even if a new memory’s mean utility <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> is not yet dominant, a large variance <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> allows it to be sampled at a high value. This grants it a chance to enter the top-<math id="S3.SS3.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> context, receive feedback, and update its posterior—forming a closed-loop evolution mechanism absent in greedy approaches.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.SS3.p9.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Semantic-Aware Initialization.</em>
To construct the initial prior for a new memory <math id="S3.SS3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{new}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{new}</annotation></semantics></math>, we transfer statistics from its <math id="S3.SS3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-nearest neighbors <math id="S3.SS3.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{n}(m_{new})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{n}(m_{new})</annotation></semantics></math> in the embedding space. The distribution is initialized as:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{new}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{j=1}^{n}\mu_{j},\quad\sigma_{new}^{2}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{j=1}^{n}\left(\sigma_{j}^{2}+(\mu_{j}-\mu_{new})^{2}\right)+\epsilon_{explore}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{new}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{j=1}^{n}\mu_{j},\quad\sigma_{new}^{2}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{j=1}^{n}\left(\sigma_{j}^{2}+(\mu_{j}-\mu_{new})^{2}\right)+\epsilon_{explore}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p9.2" class="ltx_p">Crucially, the hyperparameter <math id="S3.SS3.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{explore}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{explore}</annotation></semantics></math> acts as a "hard exploration switch," guaranteeing a non-zero variance for all new memories to ensure meaningful exposure regardless of neighbor consensus.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.SS3.p10.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Probabilistic Retrieval.</em>
During inference, given a query <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, we employ Thompson Sampling to balance exploration and exploitation. For each candidate memory <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, we sample a utility estimate <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{u}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{u}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> from its posterior:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{u}_{i}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mu_{i},\sigma_{i}^{2})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{u}_{i}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mu_{i},\sigma_{i}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p10.2" class="ltx_p">The final retrieval score fuses semantic relevance with the sampled utility:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Score}(m_{i})=(1-\lambda)\cdot\text{sim}(q_{t},m_{i})+\lambda\cdot\tilde{u}_{i}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>Score</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mtext>sim</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Score}(m_{i})=(1-\lambda)\cdot\text{sim}(q_{t},m_{i})+\lambda\cdot\tilde{u}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p10.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> balances the semantic prior and the learned utility. Unlike deterministic greedy selection, the stochasticity of <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{u}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{u}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> allows high-variance (new or uncertain) memories to occasionally override low-variance established ones, dynamically optimizing the retrieval set.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.SS3.p11.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Advantage-based Bayesian Update.</em>
To decouple memory efficacy from task difficulty (<span id="S3.SS3.p11.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Reward Bias</span>), we employ an Advantage-based feedback mechanism. We define the signal as the performance differential between memory-augmented inference (<math id="S3.SS3.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{mem}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{mem}</annotation></semantics></math>) and base inference (<math id="S3.SS3.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{base}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{base}</annotation></semantics></math>):</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{adv}=\mathcal{S}(y_{mem})-\mathcal{S}(y_{base})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{adv}=\mathcal{S}(y_{mem})-\mathcal{S}(y_{base})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p11.2" class="ltx_p">For verifiable tasks, we instantiate <math id="S3.SS3.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}(y)</annotation></semantics></math> as a binary correctness reward against ground truth, i.e., <math id="S3.SS3.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}(y)=\mathbb{I}[y\text{ is correct}]\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝕀</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> is correct</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}(y)=\mathbb{I}[y\text{ is correct}]\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This differential reward isolates the marginal contribution of the retrieved memories, cancelling out the intrinsic difficulty of the task.
Finally, we update the posterior of utilized memories via Bayesian approximation. Assuming likelihood noise variance <math id="S3.SS3.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{noise}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{noise}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, the update rules are:</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(5)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{post}=\frac{\sigma_{prior}^{2}r_{adv}+\sigma_{noise}^{2}\mu_{prior}}{\sigma_{prior}^{2}+\sigma_{noise}^{2}},\quad\sigma_{post}^{2}=\frac{\sigma_{prior}^{2}\sigma_{noise}^{2}}{\sigma_{prior}^{2}+\sigma_{noise}^{2}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{post}=\frac{\sigma_{prior}^{2}r_{adv}+\sigma_{noise}^{2}\mu_{prior}}{\sigma_{prior}^{2}+\sigma_{noise}^{2}},\quad\sigma_{post}^{2}=\frac{\sigma_{prior}^{2}\sigma_{noise}^{2}}{\sigma_{prior}^{2}+\sigma_{noise}^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p11.3" class="ltx_p">This ensures the utility converges to the true marginal gain provided by the memory, robust to heterogeneous task difficulties.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p12" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Streaming Memory Evolution and Maintenance</span>.

In a continuous task stream, an append-only memory store inevitably suffers from unbounded growth and knowledge redundancy, diluting the retrieval density of high-value insights. To maintain a compact and evolving knowledge base, we implement an online Memory Consolidation mechanism.
Immediately following the extraction phase (Sec. <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3. Memory for Verifiable Tasks ‣ 3. Methods ‣ Towards Autonomous Memory Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>), the agent performs a semantic audit by comparing the newly generated memory <math id="S3.SS3.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{new}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{new}</annotation></semantics></math> against the retrieved context set <math id="S3.SS3.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{retrieved}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{retrieved}</annotation></semantics></math> used in the current task. Acting as a curator, the LLM executes one of three discrete operations to refine the store:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p13" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S3.I5" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:0.16in;">
<li id="S3.I5.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I5.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I5.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I5.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Append (Novelty):</em> If <math id="S3.I5.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{new}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{new}</annotation></semantics></math> contains orthogonal insights not present in <math id="S3.I5.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{retrieved}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{retrieved}</annotation></semantics></math>, it is appended to the store, expanding the agent’s knowledge boundary.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I5.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I5.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I5.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I5.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Merge (Refinement):</em> If <math id="S3.I5.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{new}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{new}</annotation></semantics></math> is semantically similar to an existing memory <math id="S3.I5.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{old}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{old}</annotation></semantics></math> but offers superior clarity or generality, we replace <math id="S3.I5.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{old}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{old}</annotation></semantics></math> with a merged version, consolidating fragmented knowledge into a stronger representation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I5.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I5.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I5.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I5.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Prune (Maintenance)</em>: If <math id="S3.I5.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{new}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{new}</annotation></semantics></math> is a duplicate of an existing memory <math id="S3.I5.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{old}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{old}</annotation></semantics></math> (or if <math id="S3.I5.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{old}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{old}</annotation></semantics></math> is fully encompassed by a newly merged entry), the redundant <math id="S3.I5.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{old}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{old}</annotation></semantics></math> is removed. This keeps the retrieval space compact and ensures high information density.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4. </span>Memory for Non-Verifiable Tasks</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In non-verifiable domains (e.g., creative writing, open-ended chat, or summarization), a definitive ground truth is absent, rendering the concept of binary “success” or “failure” inapplicable. However, we posit that the core principle of memory acquisition remains the same: <span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">learning from contrast</span>. Even without a binary signal, the agent can distill actionable insights by analyzing the divergence between a higher-quality response and a lower-quality one. To this end, we replace the <span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Cost-Aware Cascade</span> used in verifiable tasks with a <em id="S3.SS4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Pairwise Contrastive Extraction</em> strategy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Pairwise Contrastive Extraction</span>.
For a given task <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, the agent generates two candidate reasoning trajectories. An external evaluator (e.g., an LLM-as-a-judge or human annotator) evaluates the pair to determine a winner, <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{\text{win}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mtext>win</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{\text{win}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and a loser, <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{\text{lose}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mtext>lose</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{\text{lose}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The agent then acts as an extractor to analyze this pair and distill the preference into a structured template:</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(6)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{\text{pref}}=\langle\text{\emph{Trigger}},\text{\emph{Dimension}},\text{\emph{Comparison}}\rangle" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>pref</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><em class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Trigger</em></mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><em class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Dimension</em></mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><em class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Comparison</em></mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{\text{pref}}=\langle\text{\emph{Trigger}},\text{\emph{Dimension}},\text{\emph{Comparison}}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S3.I6" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:0.2in;">
<li id="S3.I6.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I6.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I6.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I6.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Trigger:</em> The specific context or constraint where this preference applies (e.g., <span id="S3.I6.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“When asked to write a concise marketing email…”</span>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I6.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I6.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I6.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I6.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Dimension:</em> The evaluation axis driving the decision (e.g., <span id="S3.I6.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“Tone”</span>, <span id="S3.I6.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“Clarity”</span>, or <span id="S3.I6.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“Safety”</span>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I6.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I6.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I6.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I6.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Comparison:</em> An explicit, actionable directive derived from the contrast (e.g., <span id="S3.I6.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“Prefer direct bullet points (<math id="S3.I6.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{\text{win}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">win</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{\text{win}}</annotation></semantics></math>) over dense narrative paragraphs (<math id="S3.I6.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{\text{lose}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">lose</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{\text{lose}}</annotation></semantics></math>)”</span>).</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Relative signals are not only more robust to calibration noise but can also be used to induce a consistent preference signal in practice.
Finally, we directly reuse the SA-CTS retrieval and streaming consolidation mechanisms detailed in Sec. 3.3, where we define the advantage feedback as <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="r_{adv}=\mathbb{I}[y_{mem}\succ y_{base}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝕀</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≻</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{adv}=\mathbb{I}[y_{mem}\succ y_{base}]</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\succ" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≻</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\succ</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the LLM judge’s pairwise preference.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Datasets and Protocols</span>.
For <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">verifiable tasks</span>, we employ <em id="S4.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">AIME</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">AIME, 2025</a>)</cite>, a challenging mathematics competition dataset, where we utilize historical problems (1983–2024) for knowledge acquisition and the held-out 2025 set for evaluation; and <em id="S4.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">HotpotQA</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2018</a>)</cite>, a multi-hop reasoning benchmark, where we use the training set for memory population and report performance on the official validation set.
For <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">non-verifiable tasks</span>, following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, we select <em id="S4.SS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">AdvancedIF</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>, which evaluates the agent’s ability to follow complex, multi-constraint instructions, and the STEM subset of <em id="S4.SS1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">HelpSteer3</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>, which assesses domain-specific helpfulness in science and engineering. Detailed statistics are provided in Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.1. Experimental Setup ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ Towards Autonomous Memory Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
We provide our source code and data processing scripts in an anonymized repository<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
            Available at: <a href="https://anonymous.4open.science/r/code-release-456D/" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://anonymous.4open.science/r/code-release-456D/</a></span></span></span>.</p>
</div>
