</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baselines.</span>
We compare <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">U-Mem</span> against three categories of baselines: (1) <em id="S4.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">No Memory</em>, the frozen LLM without parameter updates or external memory, serving as the performance lower bound; (2) State-of-the-art memory frameworks, including <em id="S4.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">ReasoningBank</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, which synthesizes memories from parallel trajectories, <em id="S4.SS1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">ReMe</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cao et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, a dynamic procedural memory framework utilizing context-adaptive reuse, and <em id="S4.SS1.p2.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">MemRL</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, which employs feedback-driven Q-learning to optimize retrieval utility; and (3) <em id="S4.SS1.p2.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">RL-Based Agents</em> representing the internalization paradigm, where we employ <em id="S4.SS1.p2.1.8" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">GRPO</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2025</a>)</cite> for both verifiable tasks and non-verifiable tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation Metrics.</span>
For verifiable tasks, we report Accuracy on the test sets.
For non-verifiable tasks, following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team et al., 2026</a>)</cite> we use an LLM-as-a-judge overall score with <span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">gpt-5.1</span>. The judge outputs an aggregate rubric-based score for each response, and we report the mean normalized score (%) on the test set.</p>
</div>
