</table>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Implementation Details.</span>
We employ <em id="S4.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct</em> and <em id="S4.SS1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct</em> as the primary backbone models to evaluate the scalability of our framework across different parameter scales. All experiments are conducted on a computational node equipped with <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="3\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times</annotation></semantics></math> NVIDIA H200 GPUs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">RL Training Configurations (for Baselines).</em>
To construct competitive baselines, we implement Reinforcement Learning (RL) fine-tuning using Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) with AdamW. We apply LoRA adapters with rank <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r{=}16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r{=}16</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha{=}32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha{=}32</annotation></semantics></math>, and dropout set to <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.05</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.05</annotation></semantics></math>. Training is conducted with a learning rate of <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\mathrm{e}{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\mathrm{e}{-5}</annotation></semantics></math> over 3 epochs, utilizing a question-grouped batch size of 4 and a gradient accumulation steps of 4. We enforce a KL regularization coefficient of <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.1</annotation></semantics></math> and an advantage clipping threshold of <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.2</annotation></semantics></math>.
The reward design aligns with the task type:
(1) For <span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">verifiable tasks</span>, we use binary rewards (<math id="S4.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>) based on exact-match correctness against ground truth.
(2) For <span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">non-verifiable tasks</span>, we employ <span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Gemini-3.0-pro-preview</span> as an LLM-as-a-judge to assign rubric-based scores, which serve as the reward signal for GRPO optimization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><span id="S4.SS1.p6.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">U-Mem</span> Framework Configurations.</em>
For the autonomous memory mechanism, we configure the components as follows: (1) We utilize <span id="S4.SS1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Gemini-3.0-flash</span> as the Level-1 Teacher LLM. The Level-2 Tool-Augmented stage is supported by a secure Python code interpreter. For the Level-3 "Human Expert" proxy, we leverage <span id="S4.SS1.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Gemini-3-pro-preview</span> to simulate high-quality, authoritative feedback. (2) We constrain the memory extraction module to distill a maximum of <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=3</annotation></semantics></math> distinct insights per trajectory. (3) We employ <span id="S4.SS1.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">qwen3-4B-embedding</span> for vector representations. Retrieval is performed separately for each memory store: for verifiable tasks, we retrieve the top-<math id="S4.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=3</annotation></semantics></math> items from both the Global Procedural and Local Corrective memory bases; for non-verifiable tasks, we retrieve top-<math id="S4.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=3</annotation></semantics></math> from the Preference memory base. (4) We set the generation temperature to <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=0.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=0.7</annotation></semantics></math> during the exploration/training phase and <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=0.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=0.2</annotation></semantics></math> for final evaluation on the test set. (5) We set the exploration constant <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{explore}=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{explore}=0.1</annotation></semantics></math> and the likelihood noise variance <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{noise}^{2}=1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{noise}^{2}=1.0</annotation></semantics></math> for the Thompson Sampling mechanism. (6) We empirically set the fusion coefficient <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=0.1</annotation></semantics></math>. (7) We use <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>=10 nearest neighbors for semantic initialization.
To ensure statistical reliability, all memory-based experimental results are reported as the mean and standard deviation averaged over 5 independent runs. Prompt templates can be found in Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Prompts ‣ Towards Autonomous Memory Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>Main Results</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.1. Experimental Setup ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ Towards Autonomous Memory Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> reports the comparative performance across verifiable and non-verifiable domains.
Key observations can be summarized as:
(1) <em id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Consistent State-of-the-Art Performance:</em> <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">U-Mem</span> achieves the best performance across all datasets and model scales, significantly outperforming the "No Memory" lower bound and existing memory baselines. While passive approaches like ReasoningBank and MemRL occasionally struggle to surpass the "No Memory" baseline (e.g., ReasoningBank on AdvancedIF with Qwen2.5-14B), <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">U-Mem</span> delivers robust improvements, demonstrating the efficacy of autonomous acquisition and distributional utility modeling (via SA-CTS).
(2) <em id="S4.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Competitive Alternative to RL:</em> Most notably, <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">U-Mem</span> matches or exceeds the performance of computationally expensive RL-based agents (GRPO) without modifying model parameters. On the challenging AIME25 mathematical reasoning task (Qwen2.5-14B), <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">U-Mem</span> outperforms the RL baseline (<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="18.67\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>18.67</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">18.67\%</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="17.33\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>17.33</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">17.33\%</annotation></semantics></math>) with lower variance (<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm 1.83" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>1.83</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm 1.83</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm 2.79" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>2.79</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm 2.79</annotation></semantics></math>), suggesting that actively curated memory can serve as a more stable and cost-effective substitute for continuous fine-tuning in dynamic deployment scenarios.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3. </span>Ablation Studies</h3>

<figure id="S4.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3. </span>Component Ablations on HotpotQA (Qwen2.5-7B).</figcaption>
<table id="S4.T3.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
