<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Component Ablations</span>.

To validate the necessity of each component in <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">U-Mem</span>, we conduct an ablation study using the Qwen2.5-7B model on the HotpotQA dataset. We dissect the framework across three critical dimensions: extraction source (the cascade), retrieval strategy, and memory maintenance. The quantitative results are summarized in Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.3. Ablation Studies ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ Towards Autonomous Memory Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Efficacy of the Cost-Aware Cascade.</em>
We compare our adaptive cascade against two static baselines: <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Only Teacher</span>, which exclusively relies on the Level-1 model (lowest cost), and <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Only Human Expert</span>, which indiscriminately queries the Level-3 proxy for all failures (highest cost).
As shown in Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.3. Ablation Studies ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ Towards Autonomous Memory Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Only Teacher</span> lags significantly behind <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">U-Mem</span> (<math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="49.40\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>49.40</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">49.40\%</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="52.40\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>52.40</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">52.40\%</annotation></semantics></math>). This performance gap highlights that while Level-1 is cost-effective, it hits a capability ceiling on complex reasoning tasks; access to stronger, authoritative supervision (i.e., the Expert) is indispensable for resolving the "hard tail" of failure cases that weaker models cannot self-correct.
Conversely, <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Only Human Expert</span> achieves the performance upper bound of <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="52.80\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>52.80</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">52.80\%</annotation></semantics></math>. Crucially, <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">U-Mem</span> retains 99.2% of this upper-bound performance while invoking the expensive expert for only 23.17% of the cases. This demonstrates the cascade’s Pareto efficiency: it effectively filters out "shallow" errors solvable by weaker models, strategically reserving high-cost supervision only when essential to bridge the gap to peak performance. For a comprehensive analysis of token consumption and training time overhead, please refer to Appendix <a href="#A2.SS1" title="B.1. Cost Efficiency Analysis ‣ Appendix B Additional Experiments ‣ Towards Autonomous Memory Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Impact of SA-CTS.</em>
To isolate the contribution of our retrieval mechanism, we compare the full framework against two variants:
(1) <span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">w/o SA-CTS</span>: A vanilla RAG approach that retrieves memories solely based on static semantic similarity; and
(2) <span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">w/ MemRL’s Retrieval</span>: A deterministic utility-based baseline that ranks memories using a greedy Q-value estimate.
Results in Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.3. Ablation Studies ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ Towards Autonomous Memory Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> demonstrate the statistical superiority of <span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">U-Mem</span>.
First, it surpasses <span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">w/o SA-CTS</span> by a substantial margin of 4.6%, verifying that semantic relevance alone is insufficient—discerning <span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">functional utility</span> is essential to filter out "relevant but unhelpful" noise.
Second, <span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">U-Mem</span> outperforms the state-of-the-art <span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">MemRL</span> mechanism by 2.0%. We attribute this gain to the exploration advantage of Thompson Sampling: unlike MemRL’s greedy ranking which may permanently starve new, potentially high-value memories (the cold-start problem), SA-CTS’s probabilistic sampling ensures fair exposure for new insights, enabling continuous discovery and effective self-evolution.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Necessity of Streaming Updates.</em>
The <span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">w/o Streaming Update</span> variant adopts an append-only strategy, disabling the merge and delete operations. This results in a performance degradation of 3.2%. We attribute this decline to <span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">memory saturation</span>: without active curation, the store becomes bloated with redundant or near-duplicate entries. These redundancies dilute the information density of the top-<math id="S4.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> context window, crowding out diverse and orthogonal insights. This confirms that active memory consolidation—merging similar instances and pruning obsolete ones—is a prerequisite for sustaining long-term capability growth.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Analysis of Learning Sources and Hyperparameters</span>.

To dissect the mechanisms driving <span id="S4.SS3.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">U-Mem</span>’s performance, we investigate two pivotal design choices: the specific value of learning from failures versus successes, and the sensitivity of the system to retrieval volume (<math id="S4.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>). The quantitative results are presented in Table <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 4.3. Ablation Studies ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ Towards Autonomous Memory Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
