<div id="S4.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Efficacy of Learning from Failure.</em>
A core hypothesis of our work is that meaningful capability expansion stems primarily from correcting errors rather than merely reinforcing successes. To validate this, we compare the full hybrid framework against <span id="S4.SS3.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Success Only</span> and <span id="S4.SS3.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Failure Only</span> variants.
As shown in Table <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 4.3. Ablation Studies ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ Towards Autonomous Memory Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> (Study A), <span id="S4.SS3.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Success Only</span> yields the lowest performance (<math id="S4.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50.80\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>50.80</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50.80\%</annotation></semantics></math>). We attribute this to the fact that success-based memories largely reinforce what the model already knows, providing limited marginal gain in capability.
Conversely, <span id="S4.SS3.p6.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Failure Only</span> achieves a higher accuracy of <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="51.60\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>51.60</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">51.60\%</annotation></semantics></math>, confirming that contrastive reflection on errors effectively targets the agent’s <em id="S4.SS3.p6.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">capability boundary</em>, rectifying specific "blind spots" and misconceptions.
However, the hybrid <span id="S4.SS3.p6.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">U-Mem</span> outperforms both (<math id="S4.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="52.40\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>52.40</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">52.40\%</annotation></semantics></math>). This suggests a synergistic effect: failure-driven memories fix specific reasoning deficits (correction), while success-driven memories stabilize the execution of correct patterns on borderline tasks (consolidation), collectively forming a more robust policy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.SS3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Sensitivity to Retrieval Number (<math id="S4.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>).</em>
We analyze the impact of retrieval context size <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> on inference accuracy, with results summarized in Table <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 4.3. Ablation Studies ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ Towards Autonomous Memory Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> (Study B).
Performance initially improves as <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> increases from 1 to 3 (<math id="S4.SS3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50.20\%\to 52.40\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>50.20</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mn>52.40</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50.20\%\to 52.40\%</annotation></semantics></math>). This indicates that for multi-step reasoning tasks like HotpotQA, a single memory is often insufficient to cover the requisite evidential chain; a slightly larger context window improves informational recall.
However, performance degrades noticeably as <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> extends to 5 and 10. We attribute this drop to context dilution and noise injection: despite the utility filtering of SA-CTS, retrieving a large number of memories inevitably introduces lower-relevance or distracting information into the prompt.
Empirically, <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=3</annotation></semantics></math> strikes the optimal balance between providing sufficient guidance and maintaining a high signal-to-noise ratio.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4. </span>More Analysis</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Scalability with Experience Accumulation</span>.

A critical requirement for any learning paradigm is <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">scalability</span>: performance should improve predictably as the volume of available data increases. To verify whether <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">U-Mem</span> exhibits such scaling properties, we conduct an analysis on the HotpotQA dataset using Qwen2.5-7B. We vary the size of the source training set (experience pool) from 0 to 1,000 tasks, from which the agent autonomously extracts and populates its memory store.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.4. More Analysis ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ Towards Autonomous Memory Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we observe a clear positive correlation between the scale of the experience pool and the agent’s test accuracy. Starting from a baseline of <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="37.80\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>37.80</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">37.80\%</annotation></semantics></math>, the performance improves significantly as the agent "sees" more data, eventually peaking at <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="52.40\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>52.40</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">52.40\%</annotation></semantics></math>. Notably, the performance does not plateau or degrade significantly even as the memory pool grows larger. This indicates that <span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">U-Mem</span>’s selection mechanism (SA-CTS) effectively manages the retrieval space, allowing the agent to continuously benefit from a larger scale of experience without being overwhelmed by noise. This confirms that <span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">U-Mem</span> offers a scalable, data-driven pathway for agent evolution, akin to the scaling laws observed in pre-training but achieved non-parametrically.</p>
</div>
